1
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:16,390
The biggest fear in IELTS listening isn't that you won't understand a word,

2
00:00:16,390 --> 00:00:20,390
it's that you will miss one answer, panic, and then miss the next three.

3
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:22,450
We call this the domino effect.

4
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:28,410
The audio feels fast, not because the speakers are rushing, but because your brain is multitasking.

5
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:34,710
You are reading the next question, remembering the last one, writing an answer, and listening, all at the same time.

6
00:00:35,010 --> 00:00:37,870
If you stop for just two seconds to think about a missed

7
00:00:37,870 --> 00:00:41,070
answer, your brain stops listening to the new stream of information.

8
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:45,450
You fall behind, and once you are behind, it is almost impossible to catch up.

9
00:00:45,630 --> 00:00:49,110
Today, we are going to learn how to stop the dominoes from falling.

10
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:57,490
The first technique is psychological, the drop-it rule.

11
00:00:57,890 --> 00:00:59,930
Imagine you are trying to answer question four.

12
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:02,090
You aren't sure, you are hesitating.

13
00:01:02,430 --> 00:01:06,030
Meanwhile, the speaker has already started talking about question five.

14
00:01:06,470 --> 00:01:07,170
What should you do?

15
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:10,210
Most students try to hold on to question four.

16
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:16,070
They think, I almost have it, but while they struggle, they miss question five and six.

17
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:18,170
You must make a cold calculation.

18
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:22,350
It is better to sacrifice one point to save three points.

19
00:01:22,770 --> 00:01:25,970
If the speaker moves on, you must move on immediately.

20
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:27,910
Let's test your reflexes.

21
00:01:28,170 --> 00:01:29,870
You are stuck on question 12.

22
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:32,870
Suddenly, you hear the keyword for question 13.

23
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:34,830
What is the only safe move?

24
00:01:36,230 --> 00:01:37,190
The answer is B.

25
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:39,810
Even a quick guess takes mental energy.

26
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:43,810
If the speaker has moved to the library, your eyes must be on the library.

27
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:47,050
Forget about question 12 and focus on the next question.

28
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:53,930
But what if you get completely lost?

29
00:01:54,670 --> 00:01:57,530
You dropped a question, but you don't know where the speaker is anymore.

30
00:01:58,030 --> 00:02:00,710
This is where you need signposting language.

31
00:02:01,210 --> 00:02:04,810
Speakers use specific words to signal that they are changing topics.

32
00:02:05,230 --> 00:02:06,370
These are your GPS.

33
00:02:07,110 --> 00:02:13,310
Words like now, moving on to, on the other hand, or finally tell you exactly which question they are addressing.

34
00:02:13,830 --> 00:02:14,730
Listen to this clip.

35
00:02:15,170 --> 00:02:18,170
The speaker uses a signpost word to shift topics.

36
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:19,490
Where are we now?

37
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:23,410
The history of the castle is fascinating.

38
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:25,930
However, most visitors come for the gardens.

39
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:28,850
The signpost was however.

40
00:02:29,230 --> 00:02:31,390
This signaled a shift from history to the gardens.

41
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:34,430
If you were lost on the history question, this word tells you

42
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:36,830
to jump immediately to the gardens question.

43
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:43,960
Finally, let's talk about writing speed.

44
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:47,500
Sometimes you fall behind simply because your hand is too slow.

45
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:52,240
Writing long words like environment or necessary takes precious seconds.

46
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:55,060
During the audio, do not write the full word.

47
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:56,540
Use abbreviations.

48
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,820
You have 10 minutes at the end of the test to transfer your answers.

49
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:02,820
That is when you check your spelling and write the full word.

50
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:05,860
During the listening, just get the idea down and keep moving.

51
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:08,440
Based on this strategy, answer this.

52
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,980
Should you worry about perfect spelling while the audio is playing?

53
00:03:14,190 --> 00:03:14,610
False.

54
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:16,630
Your priority is capturing the answer.

55
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:18,730
Spelling is for the transfer time.

56
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:26,550
Speed management is about keeping your brain free.

57
00:03:26,750 --> 00:03:28,150
Let's review our key ideas.

58
00:03:28,370 --> 00:03:29,930
First, if you miss it, drop it.

59
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:32,890
Second, if you get lost, listen for signposts.

60
00:03:33,010 --> 00:03:35,290
Finally, if it's a long word, abbreviate.

61
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:37,950
Keep moving forward and you will make it to the end.

